Backport of upstream commit b91a2b4d5d84 ("wifi: rtl8xxxu: retry firmware
download on error", Soeren Moch, acked-by/signed-off-by Ping-Ke Shih --
the real mainline rtl8xxxu maintainer at Realtek -- landed via
linux-wireless Jan 2025, newer than our pinned 6.1.84 base so not present
here yet).

Our own S99zwifi-enable already documents this exact failure on real
hardware: "the chip intermittently fails USB firmware download on
power-on (rtl8xxxu_writeN: usb_control_msg -EAGAIN partway through) but a
power-cycle (gpio105 off then on) reliably recovers it", worked around by
power-cycling the whole chip and retrying from userspace. This is the
proper, narrower fix at the actual failure point instead: a single
rtl8xxxu_writeN() block-write failure during firmware download currently
aborts rtl8xxxu_init_device() immediately (device probe fails outright,
no retry at any level -- confirmed against our own tree's rtl8xxxu_core.c
before this patch). Realtek's own official/vendor driver reportedly
retries this same upload up to five times; this backport does the same:
wraps just rtl8xxxu_download_firmware() (not rtl8xxxu_start_firmware(),
which only runs once download succeeds) in a bounded retry loop.
rtl8xxxu_download_firmware() redoes its own MCU-enable/8051-reset setup
at the top on every call, so simply calling it again needs no extra
state-reset code in the retry loop itself.

Companion change: rtl8xxxu_writeN()'s "Failed to write block" print is
demoted from an unconditional dev_info to gated behind the existing
RTL8XXXU_DEBUG_REG_WRITE debug flag (already present in our tree's
rtl8xxxu.h, unrelated to this patch), so expected transient failures
during a retry don't spam dmesg.

The upstream patch also adds -std=gnu11 to that project's own standalone
out-of-tree DKMS Makefile -- not needed here: our actual in-tree kernel
Makefile already builds with -std=gnu11 (confirmed directly: "The kernel
builds with '-std=gnu11'" comment in our pinned tree's top-level
Makefile), so the for (int retry = ...) declare-in-init-statement C99
syntax already compiles as-is.

Sequenced after 0001 (rtl8188f support), since both touch
rtl8xxxu_core.c: generated by diffing the exact source at our pinned
commit (radxa/kernel, 8b0634cfd2bc551f6c0641f30df9cb03f0bd62f0) with 0001
already applied, then verified with `patch -p1 --dry-run` against that
same post-0001 state -- applies cleanly, no fuzz. Neither hunk here
overlaps anything 0001 touches in this function (0001's rtl8xxxu_init_device
hunks are all later: interrupt mask, RCR/multicast, DRIVER_EARLY_INT).

--- kernel-src-orig/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8xxxu/rtl8xxxu_core.c	2026-09-03 11:48:55.009408100 +0200
+++ kernel-src-patched/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8xxxu/rtl8xxxu_core.c	2026-09-03 11:48:55.014198580 +0200
@@ -799,9 +799,10 @@
 	return len;
 
 write_error:
-	dev_info(&udev->dev,
-		 "%s: Failed to write block at addr: %04x size: %04x\n",
-		 __func__, addr, blocksize);
+	if (rtl8xxxu_debug & RTL8XXXU_DEBUG_REG_WRITE)
+		dev_info(&udev->dev,
+			 "%s: Failed to write block at addr: %04x size: %04x\n",
+			 __func__, addr, blocksize);
 	return -EAGAIN;
 }
 
@@ -3870,8 +3871,14 @@
 	 */
 	rtl8xxxu_write16(priv, REG_TRXFF_BNDY + 2, fops->trxff_boundary);
 
-	ret = rtl8xxxu_download_firmware(priv);
-	dev_dbg(dev, "%s: download_firmware %i\n", __func__, ret);
+	for (int retry = 5; retry >= 0 ; retry--) {
+		ret = rtl8xxxu_download_firmware(priv);
+		dev_dbg(dev, "%s: download_firmware %i\n", __func__, ret);
+		if (ret != -EAGAIN)
+			break;
+		if (retry)
+			dev_dbg(dev, "%s: retry firmware download\n", __func__);
+	}
 	if (ret)
 		goto exit;
 	ret = rtl8xxxu_start_firmware(priv);
